# Supabase project settings
# Dashboard: Project Settings -> Data API (URL) / API Keys (anon key)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Never add a service-role key here with a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# This project does not use the service-role key at all: every admin
# operation runs through the signed-in admin's own session and is
# authorized by Postgres Row Level Security. If a server-only integration
# ever needs it, name it SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY (no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix)
# and only read it from server-side code (route handlers, server actions).

# The site's public origin. Used for canonical URLs, hreflang alternates,
# Open Graph/Twitter share URLs, sitemap.xml and robots.txt.
#   Production:  https://www.georepute.ai   <- set this in the host's env
#   Local dev:   http://localhost:3000
# If unset it falls back to https://www.georepute.ai, so a local build that
# leaves it blank will emit production URLs.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# Optional. The scheduling page embedded on /briefing (an inline Calendly
# widget, see components/forms/CalendlyEmbed.tsx). Must be an https
# calendly.com link, e.g. https://calendly.com/georepute/30min. Falls back to
# that same URL by default if left unset — set this to override it or point
# at a different event type. Calendly handles its own confirmation emails to
# the visitor directly, no Resend/Gmail/Google setup needed for booking.
CALENDLY_URL=

# --- Dormant: the custom name/email/message form + Google Meet automation
# that /briefing used before switching to the Calendly embed above. Not
# currently rendered anywhere, kept in the codebase (lib/actions/meeting.ts,
# lib/services/mailer.ts, lib/services/googleMeet.ts,
# lib/services/gmailSmtp.ts, components/forms/MeetingRequestForm.tsx) in
# case /briefing switches back. Safe to leave every var below unset.
#
# Powers the (currently unused) "Schedule a Meeting" form — sends the request
# via Resend (a transactional email API), not a personal mailbox password.
# Setup:
#   1. Create a free account at resend.com and generate an API key
#      (Dashboard -> API Keys). Set RESEND_API_KEY to it.
#   2. Optional: RESEND_FROM_EMAIL. Left unset, mail sends from Resend's
#      shared "onboarding@resend.dev" address, which works immediately with
#      zero extra setup but is meant for testing. For production, verify a
#      real sending domain in the Resend dashboard (e.g. georepute.ai) and
#      set this to an address on it, e.g. "GeoRepute <noreply@georepute.ai>".
# The email goes to georepute@gmail.com regardless of which domain sends it,
# unless MEETING_REQUEST_RECIPIENT is set (below), which redirects it.
# Without RESEND_API_KEY set, /briefing's form shows a clear error instead
# of silently failing.
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=

# Optional. Redirects the /briefing request email away from georepute@gmail.com,
# for testing without emailing the client. Leave unset in production. With
# Resend's shared onboarding@resend.dev sender this must be the email address of
# your own Resend account, the only one that sender can deliver to.
MEETING_REQUEST_RECIPIENT=

# Optional (dormant, see above). Powers the visitor's OWN confirmation email
# on the unused form (a copy of their request, or the Meet link once one exists).
# Without a verified sending domain, Resend above can only deliver to the
# Resend account's own address (georepute@gmail.com) — it will silently
# refuse to email an actual visitor. Setting these two sends that one email
# through Gmail's own SMTP instead, using a mailbox already owned, so it
# reaches any visitor with no domain/DNS step at all. The internal
# notification to georepute@gmail.com keeps using Resend either way.
# Setup (2 minutes, no DNS access needed):
#   1. On the georepute@gmail.com Google Account, turn on 2-Step Verification
#      if it isn't already on (myaccount.google.com/security).
#   2. Go to myaccount.google.com/apppasswords, create an app password (any
#      name, e.g. "GeoRepute website"), and copy the 16-character code shown.
#   3. Set GMAIL_SMTP_USER to georepute@gmail.com and
#      GMAIL_SMTP_APP_PASSWORD to that 16-character code (not the normal
#      Google account password — app passwords are separate and revocable).
# Leave both unset to keep the current Resend-only behavior.
GMAIL_SMTP_USER=
GMAIL_SMTP_APP_PASSWORD=

# Optional (dormant, see above). Powers the instant "Book a Google Meet call"
# option on the unused form. When all three of GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID /
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET / GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN are set, submitting it creates a
# Google Calendar event with a Meet link on the account below and emails the
# link to the visitor (and to georepute@gmail.com). Left unset, the visitor's
# preferred time is emailed to georepute@gmail.com to confirm by reply. No SDK
# is used — the Calendar REST API is called directly from
# lib/services/googleMeet.ts.
# One-time setup, signed in to the Google account that should host the calls:
#   1. console.cloud.google.com -> new project (or the existing georepute one)
#      -> APIs & Services -> Library -> enable "Google Calendar API".
#   2. OAuth consent screen / Google Auth Platform -> Audience: user type
#      External, add georepute@gmail.com (and your own address) as test users,
#      then PUBLISH the app ("In production"). While it stays in "Testing"
#      Google expires refresh tokens after 7 days. Unverified is fine for
#      internal use (click through the warning once per account).
#   3. Credentials -> Create OAuth client ID -> Web application. Add
#      https://developers.google.com/oauthplayground as an authorized redirect
#      URI. Copy the client ID and secret here.
#   4. developers.google.com/oauthplayground -> gear icon -> tick "Use your
#      own OAuth credentials" and paste the client ID/secret (re-check this
#      after signing in, before clicking Exchange — the Playground can silently
#      revert to its own client) -> authorize the scope
#      https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events, signed in as
#      georepute@gmail.com -> "Exchange authorization code for tokens" -> copy
#      the refresh token here.
# GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID is optional and defaults to the account's primary calendar.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN=
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claude API: blog draft generation (admin > New post > "Generate a draft")
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-side only. Never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_ and never commit the real
# value. Blog draft generation and blog translation both use it. The key is read in exactly one place (lib/services/claude.ts, which is
# marked server-only so the build fails if a client component imports it).
# Create a key at https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Optional. Only needed if the API answers "this API key is not scoped to a
# workspace". Best fix: create the key from inside a workspace in the Console
# (Settings > Workspaces > your workspace > API keys). Otherwise set that
# workspace's ID here (an id like wrkspc_...).
# ANTHROPIC_WORKSPACE_ID=
# Optional. Defaults to claude-opus-5. A cheaper model (e.g. claude-sonnet-5)
# can be set here without a code change; run `npm run blog:check` after changing it.
# ANTHROPIC_BLOG_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# Optional. Model for blog TRANSLATION and its independent review (admin > edit
# post > "Translate this post"). Defaults to ANTHROPIC_BLOG_MODEL. Accuracy
# matters more than cost here, so keep the strongest model you can afford.
# ANTHROPIC_TRANSLATION_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GO-LIVE CHECKLIST: what must be set in the production host's environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Values live only in the host (e.g. Vercel > Project > Settings > Environment
# Variables) and .env.local. Never commit real values.
#
#   REQUIRED
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL          https://www.georepute.ai
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL      blog, admin login
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY blog, admin login
#     ANTHROPIC_API_KEY             blog draft generation and blog translation
#                                   (a key created inside a workspace, or also set ANTHROPIC_WORKSPACE_ID)
#     RESEND_API_KEY                /briefing request emails (or the Gmail SMTP pair)
#
#   OPTIONAL
#     ANTHROPIC_BLOG_MODEL          defaults to claude-opus-5
#     ANTHROPIC_TRANSLATION_MODEL   defaults to ANTHROPIC_BLOG_MODEL
#     CALENDLY_URL                  defaults to the built-in scheduler link
#     GOOGLE_* (4 vars)             automatic Google Meet links on /briefing
#     RESEND_FROM_EMAIL, MEETING_REQUEST_RECIPIENT, GMAIL_SMTP_*
#
# After deploying: generate one draft in /admin/blogs/new and translate one post
# to confirm the key works in production, and check the function's max duration
# allows it (a translation makes 2 to 4 model calls and can take 1 to 4 minutes).

